<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zen</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zen Buddhism, Chan Buddhism, Seon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zen is a school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana Buddhism</a> that stresses meditation and direct insight over scripture. It arose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> as Chan, spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> as Seon and was brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> by Eisai and Dogen in the late 12th and 13th centuries. Patronised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> and Ashikaga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunates</a>, Zen temples shaped Japanese ink painting, gardens, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japanese-tea-ceremony">tea ceremony</a> and Noh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zen.jpg" alt="Zen" width="320" height="350" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christopher J. Fynn · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashtamangala_Lotus.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
